S[64] = "5. Results"
S[65] = "5.1 In-sample fit and parameter dispersion"
S[66] = ("Table I summarises in-sample and out-of-sample performance. Three points matter. First, median in-sample growth RMSE barely moves across M0–M4 (3.07–3.10 percentage points), so first-differenced fit does not discipline ⟦μ⟧, consistent with earlier experience with alternative capital constructions (Jorgenson and Griliches, 1967; Hulten, 1992). Second, the median level MAPE under M0 is 4.10%, implying that a re-estimated TFP path can absorb much of a level miscalibration in capital while leaving growth fit unchanged. Third, estimated lags are heterogeneous: the M1 lag has an interquartile range from 0.01 to 2.13 years, and the M2 drift ⟦μ_{1}⟧ takes both negative and positive values. A single global lag is therefore misspecified for a large part of the sample.")
S[67] = "[Table I about here]"
S[69] = "5.2 Out-of-sample evidence"
S[70] = None
S[71] = ("Figure 1 reports the out-of-sample comparison. With parameters fitted on 1970–2014 and levels predicted for 2015–2019, the median MAPE falls from 4.60% under M0 to 3.99% under M2, a 13% relative reduction. The reduction is not statistically significant: a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the 39 paired country MAPEs gives p = 0.18, and a paired percentile bootstrap interval for the country-level difference includes zero. M1 attains most of the reduction (4.06%), suggesting that recognising a lag matters more than letting it drift. M3 raises the median MAPE to 4.72%, and M4 (4.61%) is close to M0; because M4 is chosen to reconcile flow and stock accounts, its out-of-sample MAPE is not a measure of forecasting performance. Holdout inputs are treated as known, so the comparison measures specification quality rather than operational forecasting ability. M_obs attains a median MAPE of 4.17%.")
S[72] = "[Figure 1 about here]"
