S[75] = ("The median masks heterogeneity: M2 improves on M0 in 23 of 39 countries (Supplementary Figure 1), with larger gains where the asset mix shifted most towards long-gestation assets and little change where it was stable. Under M4, gains are concentrated in countries with the richest CWON produced-capital accounts.")
S[77] = ("Figure 2 compares the PIM stock ⟦K_{tang}(t;μ)+β·K_{I}(t)⟧ with CWON produced capital (NW.PCA.TO) for six countries, both demeaned within country in logs, as in the wealth loss in equation (2); raw levels differ because of price bases and units. The United States, the Republic of Korea and Israel track closely over 1995–2019; Germany and the Netherlands widen slightly after 2010; and Japan diverges after 2010, which a revaluation sensitivity check attributes plausibly to asset prices rather than quantities (Hayashi and Prescott, 2002; Supplementary Figure 4).")
S[78] = ("Because the joint estimates partly fit the wealth series, in-sample agreement is not evidence of external validity. We therefore re-estimate (⟦μ⟧, ⟦β⟧) using CWON observations for 1995–2014 and production data through 2014, fit the level normalisation on the same subsample and compare the implied capital path with the 2015–2019 CWON observations. The median holdout residual is 4.1% for the joint model, 5.0% for production-only estimates and 5.1% for M0; the joint model has a smaller residual than the production-only model in 23 of 39 countries (Supplementary Figure 14). The wealth constraint thus carries information beyond production residuals, although holdout residuals exceed the in-sample agreement, which we treat as descriptive.")
