S[86] = ("Table II summarises the cross-country distribution; country values are reported in Supplementary Table 16. Taking country means over 2010–2019, the median K gap is −4.3% (interquartile range −5.3% to −3.8%). Two kinds of interval must be distinguished. Treating the 35 countries as exchangeable draws from a superpopulation and resampling whole countries (10,000 draws, seed 20261016) gives a 95% interval for the median K gap of [−5.0%, −3.9%] and for the median TFP and labour-share shifts of [1.5, 2.1] percentage points; leave-one-country-out medians of the K gap lie between −4.31% and −4.25%. This interval reflects cross-country sampling dispersion conditional on exchangeability; it is not an interval for any country-level parameter and excludes uncertainty in the calibrated asset lags, which is bounded separately by the 0.5–2.0 scaling check. By contrast, the country ranges in Supplementary Table 16 are 2.5th–97.5th percentiles of each country's annual values within 2010–2019; they describe temporal dispersion and are not confidence intervals. The largest gaps are in Ireland (−9.2%), Costa Rica (−7.3%), the Republic of Korea (−7.1%), Slovakia (−6.7%) and Israel (−6.4%), and the smallest in Greece (−0.6%), Japan (−1.2%), Germany (−1.9%), Italy (−2.1%) and Portugal (−2.6%).")
S[87] = "[Table II about here]"
S[89] = ("__TFP and labour-share consequences.__ Because ⟦K_{obs}<K_{M0}⟧, it follows that ⟦TFP_{obs}>TFP_{M0}⟧: under the calibrated lags, the standard PIM yields log-level TFP a median 1.7 percentage points lower (4.6 points for Ireland; Figure 4). Lowering ⟦log K⟧ raises the implied labour share by approximately ⟦α×|Δlog K|⟧, which in our implementation coincides numerically with the TFP shift; the median shift is therefore also 1.7 percentage points (interquartile range 1.4–2.7; 3.1 points for the Republic of Korea; Supplementary Figure 15). These magnitudes are small relative to the secular decline in labour shares documented by Karabarbounis and Neiman (2014). They are accounting reclassifications under stated assumptions, not causal explanations of labour-share trends.")
